<p> Other home produced		unch			-2.7</p>
<p> Metals				 -1.8			-4.7</p>
<p> Chemicals			    -1.4		     -10.1</p>
<p> Other imported materials	 -1.4			-7.9</p>
<p> Total				  -1.4			-9.0</p>
<p>Consensus of economists forecasts was for input prices to fall a seasonally adjusted 0.5 percent in July. The unadjusted annual rate was forecast at down 8.8 percent year-on-year. Unadjusted output prices were forecast up 0.1 and 1.3 percent higher year-on-year.</p>
<p>The ONS said petroleum product prices rose 3.5 percent in July from June due to increases in excise duty in the July 2 budget.</p>
<p>The ONS said the increase in excise duties would have added 0.4 percent to the all-manufacturing producer prices index, but  </p>
<p>it is thought competitive pressures prevented these being passed on by manufacturers.</p>
<p>The ONS said within input prices, the biggest falls were in home-produced pigs for slaughter, down 10.5 percent, imported coffee down 12.3 percent on reduced worries of frost in Brazil, and imports of copper which fell 8.0 percent and platinum which fell 8.4 percent.</p>
